What was rewritten: the deliberation itself, which was the plugin's reason to exist. A motion, its roles, its rounds, a speech per role per round, a vote per role that a later vote replaces, a tally that adopts a motion only when more vote for than against, a rationale kept with the decision, and execution tasks parented to the adopted motion -- all of it in Common Lisp against the cell contract, as nine tools and a /agora command over one ledger file that survives a restart. The tools keep the original's names and shape (raise a motion, speak, vote, close and tally, read the result and the list, create and close the work that follows), so what a model learned to do with agora it still does here.

What was left out, and why: the Hermes runtime the original stood on. There are no Hermes profiles, so no worker templates, no per-role SOUL.md, no `hermes profile create --clone` and no per-role model pinning; a Nodecode session holds a role by calling a tool, and a team is as many sessions as the operator runs. There is no leader heartbeat and no cron, because nothing has to wake a Hermes subprocess to chair a discussion; the round is a number in the ledger, not a scheduler's turn. The dashboard tab, the REST routes and the `hermes agora` CLI are gone with the dashboard they plugged into. The kanban integration is gone too: the original layered motions over Hermes's own kanban database, while this cell keeps its own tasks, so it neither reads nor writes another application's tables -- the parent-to-child handoff the original got from kanban is the `motion_id` a task carries. Finally, nothing here spawns a subprocess or reaches the network, so an agent that speaks is one the operator started, not one the plugin started unattended.
